meta { title = "Getting started", description = "Create a native project and run a typed parallel map.", order = 3, group = "Tutorial" }

With the native toolchain installed and its bin directory on PATH, create a new project:

codeblock { language = bash, """kalandra new my-app
kalandra-check --project my-app/kalandra.toml
kalandra --project my-app/kalandra.toml -o my-app/program
./my-app/program
""" }

The program returns the ordered array [2, 4, 6]. In PowerShell, use an executable destination ending in program.exe and run it with the Windows path spelling:

codeblock { language = powershell, """kalandra --project my-app/kalandra.toml -o my-app/program.exe
./my-app/program.exe
""" }

h2 { "Read the starter" }

The starter creates kalandra.toml, src/main.k and src/math.k. Its entry imports a function and applies it using CPU workers:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "./math.k" import double

fn main() -> [int] ! TaskError:
  try parallelMap([1, 2, 3], double)
""" }

The sibling src/math.k module explicitly exports the function:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """export fn double(value: int) -> int:
  value * 2
""" }

parallelMap keeps results in input order. Its TaskError channel accounts for worker execution failures; try propagates an error to main. Selecting a Metal-capable compiler does not automatically move these CPU tasks to the GPU.

h2 { "Make a change" }

Change the multiplier in math.k, check again and rebuild. Existing compiled programs keep their compiled code until you rebuild them. Use -O2 to request optimization without changing the strict numerical rules:

codeblock { language = bash, """kalandra --project my-app/kalandra.toml -O2 -o my-app/program
""" }

callout { type = info, "The parent directory must exist, and the destination for kalandra new must not already exist, even as an empty directory. Choose a new name; the starter does not merge into existing projects." }

